      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "If I could add onto this, I had a situation where I wanted to use multiplier on an animation to control the speed of it, but the speed change was backwards. Setting a upper and lower limit of a float value for proximity would be way helpful and accomplish the same thing, if you wanted 1- to 1, or wanted -1 to 0, or even something like 0 to 1 if you need the outside of a contact to be 0 for whatever reason",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2022-05-02T20:49:20.575Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "As it is, if you have a Contact receiver set to \"Proximity\", it reports a 0-1 float value based on how close to the centre from the edge a sender is. Undoubtedly, these values are going to be used in some form of Blend Tree. The problem is, blend trees rely on / expect -1 to 1 float values to get the full range of a value. You only get 0 to 1 with Proximity. Meaning, you have to have a 2 axis puppet to get the equivalent of a 1D -1 to 1 axis and just straight up not use half of the available puppet.\nBut, if we could hit a tick box to make a “Proximity” report 0 to -1 instead of 0 to 1, and then had a pair of receivers reporting to the same float with one of them set to negative, making sure there's no instance where both receivers could be activated at the same time, you could utilize a blendtree fully/properly without having to add a ton of extra blendtree layers to get the same number of usable axis.",
  "error": null,
